# Detailed methods inserted before results by report_gate.R.
# Contents are grounded in config.R, scripts and the preserved execution logs.
heading('Methodology')
heading('Study purpose and analysis scope')
para('The purpose was to build and exercise a reproducible survival GWAS workflow on a personal computer, then evaluate whether GATE could analyse the same simulated discovery cohort. The completed extension comprises a chromosome 3 pilot, a full signal scan and a full null outcome scan. GATE was run locally under Ubuntu WSL on Windows; a computing cluster was not used.')
para('The parent simulation created a discovery cohort of 3,600 independent people and a separate replication cohort of 2,400. GATE used only the discovery cohort retained after quality control. The replication, clumping and repeated null analyses in the original Cox pipeline are separate work; they are not GATE replication or repeated GATE calibration. No Bayesian hierarchical sampler, joint longitudinal survival model or genomic structural equation model was fitted in this extension.')
heading('Simulation design and genotype generation')
para('Configuration was defined in config.R. The master random seed was 20261008; discovery and replication generation used seed plus one and seed plus two, respectively. The design comprised 22 fictional autosomes, ten linkage disequilibrium blocks per chromosome and ten variants per block, giving 2,200 variants. Variant identifiers were sim_rs followed by a five digit number. Reference and alternative alleles were A and G. Positions were fictional coordinates, with blocks separated by 200,000 bases and markers within a block separated by 1,000 bases. These are not coordinates on a human reference assembly.')
para('Block allele frequencies for Pop_A were drawn uniformly between 0.15 and 0.40. Pop_B frequencies alternated offsets of minus or plus 0.09 and were bounded between 0.07 and 0.48. Each person received two independently drawn haplotype anchors per block. At each marker, each haplotype copied its block anchor with probability 0.93 and otherwise drew a new allele from the ancestry specific frequency. Summing the two alleles gave alternative allele dosage 0, 1 or 2. This induces within block correlation but does not simulate pedigrees, realistic genome wide recombination or reference panel haplotypes.')
para('Six sites were assigned cyclically. Pop_B membership was sampled using site dependent probabilities 0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70 and 0.50. Age was uniform from 40 to 65 years and male status was Bernoulli with probability 0.45. The age covariate was age minus 50 divided by ten. Individuals were unrelated by design before deliberately copied records were added.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Survival outcomes and deliberate data faults')
para('The signal outcome followed a proportional hazards Weibull construction with shape 1.3. Site specific baseline scales were 0.025 multiplied by 0.75, 0.90, 1.00, 1.10, 1.25 or 1.40. Non genetic log hazard contributions corresponded to hazard ratios of 1.40 per ten years of age, 1.15 for male status and 1.35 for Pop_B membership. Genetic contributions were additive in G allele dosage for sim_rs00245 on chromosome 3, sim_rs00945 on chromosome 10 and sim_rs01745 on chromosome 18, with planted hazard ratios 1.50, 0.67 and 1.40.')
para('Event times were generated by inverse transformation: a negative log uniform draw was divided by the site baseline scale multiplied by the exponential of the linear predictor, then raised to the reciprocal Weibull shape. The null event time used the same non genetic structure but omitted all planted genetic effects. This is a null for genetic association, not a model without covariate effects.')
para('For each outcome, independent censoring was the earlier of five years and an exponential dropout time with rate 0.035 per year. Observed follow up was the minimum of event and censoring times. Event status was 1 when the event occurred by censoring and 0 otherwise. Signal and null outcomes had separate event and censoring draws. Time was measured from cohort entry in years; no delayed entry, competing risks, recurrent events or longitudinal measurements were simulated.')
para('Data faults were injected after generating the outcomes. Variants 1 to 10 were made monomorphic; 11 to 20 were regenerated at allele frequency 0.002; 21 to 30 were given excess heterozygosity using Bernoulli 0.90 dosages; and 31 to 40 received approximately 15 percent missingness. Background genotype missingness was 0.002. The first eight people received approximately 50 percent missing genotypes; people 9 to 12 received all heterozygous genotypes. Four genotype and phenotype records, originating from people 101 to 104, were copied with new identifiers. Raw discovery therefore contained 3,604 records rather than 3,600 unique simulated people.')
para('The simulation truth was written separately under data/truth. QC and GATE input preparation used observed data rather than truth labels. The planted identifiers were used after association testing to describe recovery. The design and implementation are recorded in scripts/simulate.R and config.R.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Sample and variant quality control')
para('scripts/qc.R first checked alignment between genotype matrix row names and phenotype identifiers and between matrix column names and variant identifiers. Identifiers had to be unique, and non missing dosages had to be 0, 1 or 2. Sample missingness and heterozygosity were computed across variants. Samples were retained only when missingness was at most 0.03, phenotype data were complete, both observed times were positive and no greater than five years, and both event indicators were 0 or 1.')
para('Heterozygosity outliers were identified within each simulated ancestry among samples passing the missingness limit. The criterion was a deviation greater than six times the larger of the ancestry specific median absolute deviation and 0.005. Exact genotype fingerprints, including missingness, were compared to flag later duplicate rows. This detects copied records; it is not a general kinship or relatedness estimator. The discovery analysis retained 3,588 people after these criteria.')
para('Variant QC was calculated after sample exclusions. Missingness had to be at most 0.02, minor allele frequency at least 0.05 and minor allele count at least 100. Allele frequency was mean observed G dosage divided by two; minor allele count was twice the non missing sample count multiplied by minor allele frequency. Hardy Weinberg equilibrium used a two sided conditional exact test separately in Pop_A and Pop_B. The smaller within ancestry p value had to be at least 1e-6. This avoided applying an equilibrium filter to a pooled mixture of ancestry groups. The retained discovery panel contained 2,160 variants.')
heading('Principal components and covariate preparation')
para('scripts/pca.R pruned markers sequentially within chromosome and a 250,000 base window. For pruning correlations, each genotype was centred on its ancestry specific mean and missing centred entries were set to zero. A marker was retained only when squared correlation with every previously retained nearby marker was below 0.20. The discovery PCA panel contained 216 markers. This panel was for PCA; it was not substituted for the full marker panel used by the GATE relationship matrix.')
para('For PCA, retained dosages were centred on their pooled mean and divided by the square root of twice the allele frequency multiplied by one minus that frequency. Missing centred values were set to zero. prcomp used no additional centring or scaling and extracted three components; their sample scores were then standardised. load_analysis checked exact ID order before joining PC1, PC2 and PC3 to the QC phenotype data. GATE adjusted for these PCs as well as explicit ancestry and site indicators.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('GATE input export and independent validation')
para('scripts/prepare_gate.R exported the discovery QC matrix into SNP major PLINK BED, BIM and FAM files, a VCF and a tab separated phenotype file. The BED header was bytes 108, 27 and 1. With BIM A1 set to alternative G and A2 to reference A, two bit codes were 00 for dosage 2, 10 for dosage 1, 11 for dosage 0 and 01 for missing. Every exported genotype was decoded and compared with its original value and missingness, not merely a sample of rows.')
para('BIM records contained chromosome, variant ID, zero genetic distance, fictional position, G and A. FAM used the person ID for both family and individual identifiers, zero parental IDs, sex code zero and phenotype minus nine. Sex in FAM was a placeholder; the actual male covariate came from the phenotype table. The PLINK unknown sex warning therefore did not mean that the model omitted sex.')
para('The phenotype table contained id, time, event, null_time, null_event, age10, male, PC1, PC2 and PC3, plus siteSite_2 through siteSite_6 and ancestry_groupPop_B. Indicators were generated with model.matrix after removing its intercept column. Reference groups were Site_1 and Pop_A. The model contained eleven adjustment columns. IDs and sample order were maintained across export formats.')
para('VCF records used REF A, ALT G and the GT field, with 0/0, 0/1, 1/1 or ./. for the four dosage states. The exported file was compressed with bgzip and indexed for regional access. An initial TBI index allowed tabix to list chromosomes but GATE failed to open the requested region and reported a sample count mismatch. Replacing it with a CSI index using tabix -f -C -p vcf resolved the reader failure. The headerless sample file was extracted from the phenotype IDs in their exported order.')
para('Independent validation used PLINK --bfile data/gate/discovery --freq --out results/gate/plink_input_check. scripts/check_gate_plink.R matched variant IDs and reoriented PLINK frequencies to G when its minor allele label differed. Frequencies had to agree with the original dosage frequencies within 1e-4, allowing for printed rounding. Observed allele counts had to equal twice the number of non missing original dosages for every variant. Both checks passed. The BED round trip, PLINK checks and later GATE output checks provide separate checks of coding and alignment.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Runtime installation and source provenance')
para('The project remained at D:/survival_gwas_simulation on the Toshiba external drive. Preflight inspection found Windows R and an Ubuntu WSL installation, but no callable GATE environment. Installation proceeded after the user authorised it. A separate micromamba environment was used rather than replacing the system R library. Initial extraction directly to the NTFS drive through WSL was too slow for the many dependency files.')
para('An 8 GB ext4 filesystem image was therefore created at tools/gate-runtime.ext4 on the same Toshiba drive, labelled GATE_RUNTIME and mounted at /mnt/gate-runtime. The environment prefix was /mnt/gate-runtime/env and the micromamba root was /mnt/gate-runtime/mamba. scripts/mount_gate_runtime.sh checks the expected label and creates the image only if absent. Existing images are mounted rather than reformatted. WSL restarts can remove this mount, so root wrapper scripts mount the image and run the requested build or analysis in the same active WSL invocation.')
para('Interrupted extraction also caused invalid copies in the intermediate package cache. The original 122 package archives were checked for archive integrity, copied into the image and hashed with SHA256 and MD5. The installer wrote explicit local archive URLs with MD5 hashes and linked all 122 packages successfully. Evidence is preserved in cache-integrity.json, archive-checksums.json and the environment installation logs under logs/gate. Local explicit URLs depend on the preserved archives and mounted path; the manifest alone is not a portable remote lock file.')
para('The final analysis environment used R 4.2.3, Python 3.10.22, GCC family compilers 15.3.0 and CMake 3.31.8. Important R dependencies included Rcpp 1.0.12, RcppArmadillo 0.12.8.4.0, RcppParallel 5.1.6, RcppEigen 0.3.4.0.0, Matrix 1.6.5, SPAtest 3.1.2, SKAT 2.2.5, MetaSKAT 0.82 and optparse 1.7.5. cget 0.2.0 and lintools were also installed. bgzip and tabix came from htslib 1.24. The complete package listing is in logs/gate/environment-explicit.txt; the loaded R session is in package-load.txt and session.txt.')
para('The upstream GATE archive was preserved as tools/GATE-main.zip and pinned to commit 962f1028120c73e24ec34f29e02eeae3bdc3bfb3. The build source was extracted inside the image. Extraction omitted version control history, prebuilt dependency and BGEN build directories, and most Boost documentation and test material, while retaining required headers and source. The installed package reported version 0.45. The package was accepted only after R CMD INSTALL completed and library(GATE) succeeded from the final installation.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Build failures and compatibility changes')
para('The upstream source retained SAIGE DLL lookup and initialisation names despite Package being GATE. scripts/gate_build_compat.py changed useDynLib(SAIGE) to useDynLib(GATE), R wrapper PACKAGE strings to GATE and R_init_SAIGE to R_init_GATE. Exported native function symbols remained consistently registered; statistical routines were not intentionally changed. Original files and the list of changes were preserved in logs/gate/upstream_before_compat and build_compat.json.')
para('The native build first failed in the pinned older SuperLU source because GCC 15 interpreted an empty C parameter list under its newer default standard differently. The configure C flags were changed from -fPIC to -fPIC -std=gnu11. A second failure arose because undeclared legacy function calls were errors under the newer compiler. Adding -Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration allowed those calls to remain warnings, matching the older build expectation. This was a compatibility allowance, not evidence that compiler warnings are irrelevant. SuperLU then compiled.')
para('The configure script originally removed thirdParty/cget at each retry. That removal was replaced with directory creation so successfully compiled dependencies were retained. Its pinned inputs included statgen/savvy v2.1.0 and SuperLU commit b6177d0b743c0f1f6765db535dd8b6ce30c00061. cget invoked four build jobs using the detected CPU count; setting MAKEFLAGS alone did not limit those jobs. The BGEN Waf build was separately limited to one job.')
para('BGEN used older Python build files. Its wscript files were converted with lib2to3, and Python 3.10 was retained because the old Waf code depended on imp, absent from Python 3.12. Waf next failed when a generator explicitly raised StopIteration; that statement in its extracted Node.py was changed to return. BGEN then failed on std::ios::streampos, which was replaced with the standard std::streampos type. BGEN linked successfully after those changes, followed by GATE compilation and package-load checks.')
para('R C++ optimisation was set to -O1 -g0 for CXX, CXX11 and CXX14 in config/gate.Makevars to limit compilation memory. R startup was isolated using --vanilla and, in WSL, R_ENVIRON_USER and R_PROFILE_USER set to /dev/null. Build threads used OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 and OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1. Failed attempt logs and the final package-build.log were retained. These checks establish successful build and execution for this environment; they do not establish byte identical binaries across platforms.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Survival null models and relationship matrix')
para('GATE is Genetic Analysis of Time to Event phenotypes. Its published approach performs single variant survival association testing using a frailty model and saddlepoint approximation to improve score test tail probabilities under censoring [1, 2]. In this analysis the adjustment set was age10, male, PC1, PC2, PC3, five site indicators and the Pop_B indicator. A common baseline with fixed indicator effects was used. A genetic relationship matrix represented the random genetic component; no separate sparse relationship matrix was supplied.')
para('The relationship matrix used the QC PLINK panel with the logged default minMAFforGRM of 0.01. Since exported variants had already passed MAF 0.05, the full 2,160 marker panel was eligible. IsSparseKin and useSparseGRMtoFitNULL were FALSE. The PCA pruned panel was not used as the relationship panel. LOCO was FALSE in both fitting and testing, so the chromosome being tested remained represented in the relationship matrix. This is a pilot choice and may allow tested regions to contribute to the fitted background genetic component.')
para('The signal model used --phenoCol=event and --eventTimeCol=time. The null outcome model used null_event and null_time. Both used --traitType=survival, --sampleIDColinphenoFile=id and the same covariate list. Raw times were retained without an event time bin size override. No inverse normal transformation was requested. The log confirms all 3,588 exported samples had usable phenotypes and that GATE performed its default QR transformation of covariates. The initial binomial fit printed in the log is an internal initialisation step; the requested final trait type remained survival.')
para('Explicit fitting settings were two threads, maximum 100 outer iterations and LOCO FALSE. Logged defaults were outer tolerance 0.02, PCG tolerance 1e-5, maximum 500 PCG iterations, trace coefficient of variation cutoff 0.0025 and variance ratio coefficient of variation cutoff 0.001. Thirty random markers were requested for variance ratio estimation. The logs show the ratio CV met its cutoff after 30 markers for each outcome. No additional command line random seed was imposed for GATE; preserving the fitted model files is therefore part of the execution record rather than claiming that a fresh fit must be bit identical.')
para('Before association testing, scripts/check_gate_null.R loaded the saved modglmm object, required converged=TRUE and traitType=survival, checked the sample count and equality of ID sets, required finite non negative variance components and required a finite positive variance ratio. The surviving model and ratio files were results/gate/discovery_signal.rda and discovery_signal.varianceRatio.txt, with analogous discovery_null files. Passing these checks permitted testing; it was not interpreted as proof of model adequacy.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Pilot testing and full association scans')
para('The first association trial used chromosome 3 and its 100 retained variants. GATE read the indexed VCF GT field, the headerless sample list, the fitted signal model and its variance ratio file. After the CSI fix, the reader reported 3,588 samples and 100 passing markers. The same fitted signal model was then used for a full scan. A separate null outcome survival model was fitted and checked before its scan. scripts/run_gate_full.sh ran step2_SPAtests.R for chromosomes 1 through 22 for both outcomes, producing 44 chromosome files.')
para('Single variant eligibility was minMAF=0.05 and minMAC=100. LOCO remained FALSE. Missing dosages were not dropped, as confirmed by IsDropMissingDosages=FALSE; mean imputation retained all participants. No imputed continuous dosages were supplied and no external imputation quality threshold beyond the logged minInfo=0 was applied. The output imputationInfo value of one does not represent empirical imputation accuracy: these data were exported hard call genotypes. No group, burden, conditional or rare variant analysis was requested.')
para('For each marker the output retained chromosome, position, identifier, Allele1, Allele2, allele count, allele frequency, sample size, coefficient, standard error, score statistic, final p value, normal approximation p value, SPA convergence flag and variance quantities. G was confirmed as Allele2. Exponentiating the GATE coefficient gave the reported per allele hazard ratio estimate. p.value was the association measure used for significance; p.value.NA was retained as a normal approximation diagnostic.')
para('The SPA cutoff was two standardised score units. scripts/summarise_gate.R identified required SPA tests using the absolute score divided by the square root of varT greater than two and required their convergence flag to be one. A zero convergence flag for a marker below that cutoff was not labelled a failed SPA fit. This distinction prevented ordinary normal approximation tests from being counted as convergence failures.')
heading('Multiple testing and diagnostic summaries')
para('The panel family wise threshold was 0.05 divided by 2,160 planned tests, approximately 2.3148e-5, separately for each outcome. The conventional 5e-8 threshold was also reported. Counts below either threshold are counts of correlated markers, not independent loci. The QQ plot compared ordered p values with the uniform reference plotting positions from ppoints. Lambda GC was the median one degree of freedom chi square statistic obtained from upper tail p values divided by the median of its null chi square distribution. No genomic control adjustment was applied to the p values.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Output checks and Cox comparison')
para('scripts/summarise_gate.R combined the 22 whitespace separated chromosome files per outcome and required thirteen checks: total variant count, unique variant IDs, membership in the QC ID list, participant count, chromosome, position, reference allele, effect allele, allele frequency, finite coefficients and positive standard errors, finite p values in the interval above zero through one, finite positive score variance, and convergence for required SPA tests. GATE G frequencies had to agree with original observed dosage frequencies within 1e-6. Validated combined outputs were written as tab separated signal_all.tsv and null_all.tsv.')
para('The matched comparison was constructed in scripts/gate_cox_comparator.R for chromosome 3 only. Each variant used survival::coxph with observed signal time and status, G dosage, age10, male, PC1 to PC3, site and ancestry_group, a common baseline, and Efron handling of tied times. Missing G dosages were filled by that variant mean. Wald p values were calculated from coefficient divided by standard error. The comparison joined by variant ID and required matching G effect alleles. Its 100 variant data are in chr3_comparison.tsv.')
para('The original Cox discovery scan differed: it used strata(site, ancestry_group) for separate baseline hazards and excluded missing genotypes per marker. The matched comparison was created to reduce those two implementation differences, but it did not make the methods identical. GATE retained its random genetic component, score based inference and SPA tail calculation. The comparison was descriptive, without a prespecified acceptable correlation or equivalence test. The GATE null outcome was not given a separate matched Cox scan in this extension.')
heading('Calibration limits and unperformed work')
para('The GATE evaluation used one signal dataset and one null dataset. The twenty repeated null simulations reported in the original Cox pipeline were not repeated with GATE and do not validate GATE error rates. No GATE analysis of the independent replication cohort, related family data, a million variant dataset or an alternative LOCO fit was performed. The unrelated simulation and small correlated marker panel cannot establish the benefits of a frailty model in a biobank.')
para('Below one lambda values are evidence of descriptive deflation in these runs. Possible contributions include the simulated marker and ancestry structure and the non LOCO relationship matrix, but the present comparison does not isolate a cause. Convergence and zero significant null results do not alone demonstrate nominal type I error, particularly at extreme p value thresholds. Production work should assess repeated null outcomes, a suitable relationship marker set, LOCO behaviour, relatedness, model assumptions and independent replication before interpreting discoveries.')
doc <- body_add_break(doc)
heading('Automation reporting and reproducibility record')
para('The original simulation, QC, PCA, Cox scans, validation and reports are organised in the project Snakefile and R scripts. The GATE extension was executed by shell scripts and R validation scripts; it was not added as a Snakemake target. All runtime files remain in the Toshiba backed image. GATE wrappers mount it with WSL root and then run the analysis as the ordinary vusi user. WSL must be available and the Toshiba drive must have the expected D drive assignment.')
para('Input preparation is scripts/prepare_gate.R. Independent export validation is scripts/check_gate_plink.R. Installation is recorded in scripts/install_gate_image.py, scripts/build_gate.sh and scripts/gate_build_compat.py. scripts/resume_gate_build.sh mounts the runtime and invokes the build. scripts/resume_gate_pilot.sh runs the chromosome 3 pilot and signal model fit. scripts/resume_gate_full.sh validates the existing signal fit, fits the null outcome model and launches both full scans. The full wrapper therefore assumes that the pilot has already created valid compressed inputs and the signal model.')
para('From the project directory, the pilot wrapper is invoked as: wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -u root --exec bash /mnt/d/survival_gwas_simulation/scripts/resume_gate_pilot.sh. The full scan uses the same command with resume_gate_full.sh. These commands rerun analysis; regenerating raw data or changing phenotype or covariate inputs requires refitting the pilot signal model before the full wrapper. The command log and configuration are the authority for the completed run.')
para('After scan completion, native Windows R 4.5.1 ran Rscript --vanilla scripts/summarise_gate.R and then scripts/report_gate.R. The reporting script uses officer, flextable and ggplot2 and does not require Pandoc. The separate scripts/gate_methodology.R supplies this detailed methods section. The reporting R session is saved in report/gate/R_session.txt. Tables remain editable in Word and the three PNG plots are embedded at 200 dpi. This supplement does not create a new gtsummary cohort table; the parent report contains that table.')
para('The packaged DOCX renderer was attempted but LibreOffice was unavailable. Microsoft Word exported the document to PDF for internal layout verification, and bundled Python PDF rendering produced page images for visual inspection. The previous three page report was preserved as report/GATE_Survival_GWAS_Results_before_methods.docx. The expanded file uses the same requested filename. This documentation update does not change the stored association results.')
heading('Evidence files and primary references')
para('Prepared inputs and round trip checks: data/gate. Independent PLINK evidence: results/gate/plink_input_check.frq and plink_validation.txt. Fitted models and model checks: results/gate/discovery_signal and discovery_null prefixes. Association summaries and thirteen check tables: results/gate/summary.tsv, signal_checks.tsv and null_checks.tsv. Build and model logs: logs/gate. Plot sources: report/gate. These relative paths are under D:/survival_gwas_simulation.')
para('[1] GATE source and user documentation: https://github.com/weizhou0/GATE. The archived commit and local execution logs determine the version and options used here; current web documentation can differ from the archived source.')
para('[2] GATE method paper, Nature Communications 2022: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-022-32885-x. Statistical descriptions are summarised in this report; software options and validation claims are grounded in the local scripts and logs.')
